India's food delivery ecosystem is about to face three simultaneous shocks - rising petrol prices,
the 2026–27 Super El Niño bringing record heatwaves and erratic monsoons, and surging LPG and cooking oil costs hitting restaurant partners. 
None of Swiggy's current systems read these external economic signals and act on them proactively.
CrisisIQ is an AI informational layer built on Swiggy's MCP platform that serves three audiences through three separate modules.
Riders get quarterly bike servicing vouchers when the PPAC fuel quarterly average crosses ₹10 above the baseline - simple, predictable, no per-delivery math.
Restaurant partners get 3–4 week commodity cost forecasts from AGMARKNET data, a private shrinkflation monitor, and opt-in group buying coordination.
Customers get a tap-to-expand breakdown of what already exists inside their delivery charges - no new fees, just honest transparency.
The project was designed compliance-first against Swiggy's Builders Club published rules - zero new charges, zero ranking changes, zero dark patterns,
with every prohibited conduct rule mapped explicitly. External data comes entirely from open Indian government APIs: PPAC for fuel, IMD for climate, AGMARKNET for commodities.
